---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
const example = `<div class="example">
  <input id="reduce" type="checkbox">
  <label for="reduce">Reduce motion in this demonstration</label>
  <input id="toggle" type="checkbox">
  <label for="toggle">Change the decorative swatch</label>
  <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
</div>
<style>
  .swatch { opacity: .35; transition: opacity
    calc(300ms * var(--udx-motion-scale, 1)); }
  #toggle:checked ~ .swatch { opacity: 1; }
  #reduce:checked ~ .swatch { transition-duration: 0s; }
</style>`;
---
<MotionPage title="Motion: accessibility and reduced motion" description="The reduced-motion policy of @udixio/motion, what each verb shows under it, and the focus, pause and reading rules that the runtime keeps.">
  <h1>Accessibility and reduced motion</h1>
  <p>The runtime follows the reduced-motion policy of the document. The system preference <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>, or <code>&lt;html data-motion-policy="reduce"&gt;</code>, selects it. An absent attribute, <code>auto</code>, or an unknown value follows the system preference. The document stylesheet defines <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> as 1 by default, and as 0 under the reduced policy.</p>

  <h2>What each verb shows under reduced motion</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Under reduced motion</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>appear</code>, <code>disappear</code></td><td>A short opacity fade. An exit with <code>exit: 'out-of-flow'</code> is in place.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>emphasize</code></td><td>Not animated: the element applies its end frame.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>reveal</code></td><td>The element is shown at once, unless its definition sets another <code>reduced</code> value.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scrub</code></td><td>The element stays still; the scroll link is stopped.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loop</code></td><td>The loop does not play: the element keeps its stylesheet state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>layout</code></td><td>The new layout applies at once.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gestures</td><td>The release is written at once, with no glide.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Page transitions</td><td><code>navigate()</code> runs the update alone.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Text</td><td>The text is not split: the element plays whole.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scroll</td><td><code>scrollTo</code> jumps to its destination.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Values</td><td>A smoothed value is written at once; a <code>time</code> value holds the first end of its range.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p><code>--udx-motion: none</code> on an element or an ancestor applies the end state without animating, whatever the policy. Use it per breakpoint with Tailwind: <code>md:[--udx-motion:none]</code>.</p>
  <p>Multiplying a transition duration by <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> alone does not make every effect safe. Adapt or remove translation and loops as appropriate, and keep essential content available. A raw provider does not apply this policy.</p>

  <h2>Focus when an element leaves (WCAG 2.4.3)</h2>
  <p>An exit sets <code>inert</code> on its element. When the exit starts with focus inside it, <code>restoreFocus</code>, or <code>data-motion-restore-focus="&lt;selector&gt;"</code> in HTML, names the element that takes focus first. See <a href="/motion/presence">Presence</a>.</p>

  <h2>Pausing a loop (WCAG 2.2.2)</h2>
  <p>A loop never ends, so each loop states in its definition how it is paused: <code>pause: 'control'</code> when the page offers a control, or <code>pause: 'essential'</code> with a reason when the loop is the content itself. See <a href="/motion/reveal-scrub-loop">Reveal, scrub and loop</a>.</p>

  <h2>Reading and keyboard</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A counter holds its number in an <code>aria-hidden</code> span while it counts, and the final number in a visually hidden span, so a screen reader reads the final number.</li>
    <li>A split text keeps its semantics, and its visual copy is <code>aria-hidden</code>: the text is read once, as written (see <a href="/motion/text">Text</a>).</li>
    <li>Every gesture has a keyboard alternative, and a reorder announces each step in a live region (see <a href="/motion/gestures">Gestures</a>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it without JavaScript</h2>
  <p>The checkbox state remains visible and the final appearance is available without animation. The reduction checkbox only changes this demonstration, not your system or document policy.</p>
  <div class="demo not-prose">
    <input id="demo-reduce" type="checkbox" /> <label for="demo-reduce">Reduce motion in this demonstration</label>
    <input id="demo-toggle" type="checkbox" /> <label for="demo-toggle">Change the decorative swatch</label>
    <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>
  <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
  <h2>Shadow DOM</h2>
  <p>The HTML selector does not cross a shadow boundary. The scale set by the document stylesheet can be inherited into a shadow root, but a local override can defeat it. Without the document stylesheet, the HTML attribute alone does not guarantee reduction inside a shadow root.</p>
  <p>Search has its own behavior: it respects the system preference even without the stylesheet, has a 200 ms and easing fallback, and reads the attribute in ordinary DOM. Do not generalize this behavior to all components or providers.</p>
  <style>
    .demo { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; align-items: center; gap: .75rem; padding: 1.5rem; border: 1px solid currentColor; border-radius: 1rem; }
    .demo input { width: 1.25rem; height: 1.25rem; accent-color: var(--color-primary); }
    .demo input:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid currentColor; outline-offset: 3px; }
    .swatch { grid-column: 1 / -1; width: 5rem; height: 3rem; border-radius: .75rem; background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35; transition: opacity calc(300ms * var(--udx-motion-scale, 1)); }
    #demo-toggle:checked ~ .swatch { opacity: 1; }
    #demo-reduce:checked ~ .swatch { transition-duration: 0s; }
  </style>
</MotionPage>
